- What drift-detection metrics would you track and how would you trigger retraining?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a practical, on-device ML flow that balances accuracy, size, privacy, and deployment realities.

## Key Concepts
- On-device constraints: memory, CPU, quantization
- Feature engineering: cyclical time, geospatial bucketing, hashed categories
- Model choice: compact linear models with proper regularization
- Privacy-aware validation: leave-one-user-out or per-session splits
- Evaluation: AUROC, calibration, appropriate thresholds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny logistic regression dot-product (illustrative)
function predict(w, x) {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) s += w[i] * x[i];
  return 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-s));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle missing values on-device?
- How would you validate robustness to distribution shifts after deployment?

## Why This Is Asked
A beginner-safe way to ensure models stay reliable in production, focusing on data drift and quick retraining.

## Key Concepts
- Data drift vs concept drift
- KS statistic, rolling window evaluation
- Lightweight retraining, canary/shadow deployment
- Feature versioning and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// drift check scaffold (pseudo)
function ksTest(a, b) { /* returns p-value */ }
function driftFeatures(train, prod, thresh=0.05) {
  const drift = {};
  for (const key of Object.keys(train)) {
    drift[key] = ksTest(train[key], prod[key]);
  }
  return Math.max(...Object.values(drift)) < thresh;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you set thresholds for drift in a new product line?
- How would you extend this to multiclass targets or time-series features?
